# Line-ending policy.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the Git for Windows default, and without this file
# it rewrites checked-out files to CRLF. That is harmless for ordinary source,
# but it silently breaks the L3 codegen snapshot tests, because BOTH sides of
# those tests are files in this repo:
#
#   * the goldens, read off disk and compared byte-for-byte, and
#   * the minijinja templates, whose bytes `include_dir!` bakes into the
#     binary at compile time, and which therefore become the generator's
#     output verbatim.
#
# Pinning only the goldens is worse than pinning neither: the generator picks
# up CRLF from a translated template while the golden stays LF, so every
# snapshot fails by exactly one byte per template line, as a whole-file diff
# that looks like a content change and is not. Pin both sides rather than
# teach the comparison to normalize; the goldens exist to catch byte-level
# drift, so nothing may rewrite them. `embedded_templates_are_lf_only` in
# sb-codegen fails loudly if the template half of this ever comes undone.

# Sensible default: git decides text vs binary, normalizes to LF in the repo.
* text=auto

# Both sides of the codegen snapshot comparison, plus the committed input the
# goldens are rendered from. Never translate these on checkout, on any
# platform.
crates/sb-cli/tests/level3_goldens/** text eol=lf
crates/sb-codegen/templates/** text eol=lf
crates/sb-cli/tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# Shipped message definitions and the shell installers are consumed by
# protoc/flatc and by bash; keep them LF everywhere.
messages/**/*.proto text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts stay CRLF so cmd.exe/PowerShell hosts read them cleanly.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
